      "concern": "The SDD contains contradictory concurrency-semaphore decisions: §5.6 says cross-process N-slot semaphore is always introduced by default, while §1.4.4 and §8 T2.14 still say the module is conditional and may not be introduced if stress tests pass; §10.1 also preserves an obsolete `threading.Semaphore` default / flock opt-in statement.",
      "severity": "HIGH",
      "severity_score": 770,
      "why_matters": "Conflicting implementation guidance can cause the safety-critical cross-process concurrency limiter to be omitted or made opt-in, recreating the session-state corruption risk the revision claims to close.",
      "location": "§1.4.4 Headless adapter concurrency semaphore; §5.6 Concurrency semaphore design; §8 Phase 2 T2.14; §10.1 Resolved questions Q6",
      "recommendation": "Normalize all sections to one authoritative decision: cross-process N-slot semaphore is mandatory/default, process-local throttling is secondary, and FR-8.6 only determines per-CLI N values rather than whether the module exists.",
      "source": "tertiary_skeptic"
    },
    {
      "id": "SKP-002",
      "concern": "The `auto` algorithm compares auth_type buckets keyed by the same `model_id`, but headless and HTTP/API paths appear to use different model IDs such as `claude-headless` versus `claude-opus-4-7`; no model-family or equivalence mapping is defined.",
      "severity": "CRITICAL",
      "severity_score": 875,
      "why_matters": "Auto-mode may never have comparable headless and http_api samples for the same `(role, model_id, provider)` tuple, forcing cold-start behavior or biased single-bucket selection indefinitely. This undermines G-4 measurement, rollout decisions, and the claimed deterministic empirical routing.",
      "location": "§5.2 `read_per_auth_type_window(role, model_id, provider, ...)`; §3.2 model-config examples; §1.5 Data Flow",
      "recommendation": "Introduce an explicit comparison key such as `model_family`, `dispatch_group`, or `advisor_strategy_group` in model-config, and have MODELINV aggregation compare auth_types within that group rather than exact model_id. Add tests with distinct headless/http_api model IDs in the same comparison group.",
      "source": "tertiary_skeptic"
    },
    {
      "id": "SKP-004",
      "concern": "The fail-closed redaction pipeline captures subprocess stdout/stderr before redaction and only then truncates or scrubs, leaving large or secret-bearing outputs resident in process memory and risking memory exhaustion before the >1MB truncation rule applies.",
      "severity": "HIGH",
      "severity_score": 735,
      "why_matters": "A misbehaving CLI or model can emit unbounded stderr/stdout containing tokens or binary data. Capturing it fully with `communicate()` can OOM the process, hang cleanup, or expand the blast radius of secret material before the redaction boundary.",
      "location": "§1.9.1 Fail-closed redaction pipeline; §5.4 `probe_cli`; §6.3 Logging strategy",
      "recommendation": "Implement bounded streaming reads with a hard byte cap before buffering, redact incrementally or from capped temp files with mode 0600, and enforce limits at the pipe-read boundary rather than after `communicate()` returns.",
      "source": "tertiary_skeptic"
    },
    {
      "id": "SKP-005",
      "concern": "The redaction invariant states raw model output never reaches stdout unless redacted, but the dispatch path must return raw model output to skill callers to function; the boundary between caller response and audit/doctor output is ambiguous.",
      "severity": "HIGH",
      "severity_score": 710,
      "why_matters": "If implemented literally, model completions may be redacted before being returned to skills, breaking normal operation. If implemented loosely, developers may accidentally log raw output to audit because the invariant is not scoped precisely.",
      "location": "§1.9.1 Fail-closed redaction pipeline; §1.5 Data Flow; §5.5 auto_selection_inputs redaction policy",
      "recommendation": "Define separate data boundaries: raw completion may be returned in-memory to the immediate caller, but any persisted log, journal, doctor stdout, or audit envelope must pass through fail-closed redaction. Add tests proving raw completion is not persisted while caller response remains intact.",
